Article 7 An intellectual property right holder may, in accordance with the provisions of these Regulations, apply to the General Administration of Customs for the recordal of his intellectual property rights; if he applies for recordal, he shall submit a written application. The application shall include the following contents:
(1) the name or title, place of registration or nationality of the intellectual property right holder, etc.;
(2) the name, content and relevant information of the intellectual property right;
(3) the status of exercise of licenses for the intellectual property right;
(4) the name, place of origin, Customs of entry or exit, importers or exporters, main features, price, etc. of the goods in which the intellectual property right holder lawfully exercises his intellectual property rights;
(5) the names of manufacturers, importers or exporters, Customs of entry or exit, main features, price, etc. of the goods that are known to infringe the intellectual property right.
If there are certifying documents for the contents of the application specified in the preceding paragraph, the intellectual property right holder shall attach them.

Article 8 The General Administration of Customs shall, within 30 working days from the date of receipt of all application documents, make a decision on whether to approve the recordal, and notify the applicant in writing; if it decides not to approve the recordal, it shall state the reasons.
The General Administration of Customs shall not approve the recordal under any of the following circumstances:
(1) the application documents are incomplete or invalid;
(2) the applicant is not the intellectual property right holder;
(3) the intellectual property right is no longer protected by laws or administrative regulations.

Article 13 Where an intellectual property right holder requests the Customs to detain goods suspected of infringement, he shall submit an application and relevant supporting documents, and provide evidence sufficient to prove that the facts of infringement are obviously present.
The application shall include the following main contents:
(1) the name or title, place of registration or nationality of the intellectual property right holder, etc.;
(2) the name, content and relevant information of the intellectual property right;
(3) the names of the consignee and the consignor of the goods suspected of infringement;
(4) the name, specifications, etc. of the goods suspected of infringement;
(5) the ports, time, means of transport, etc. at or through which the goods suspected of infringement may be imported or exported.
If the goods suspected of infringement are suspected of infringing a recorded intellectual property right, the application shall also include the Customs recordal number.

Article 24 In any of the following circumstances, the Customs shall release the detained suspected infringing goods:
(1) where the Customs detains suspected infringing goods in accordance with Article 15 of these Regulations and does not receive a notice of assistance in enforcement from the People's Court within 20 working days from the date of detention;
(2) where the Customs detains suspected infringing goods in accordance with Article 16 of these Regulations and does not receive a notice of assistance in enforcement from the People's Court within 50 working days from the date of detention, and cannot determine through investigation that the detained suspected infringing goods infringe intellectual property rights;
(3) where the consignee or consignor of goods suspected of infringing a patent right provides a security deposit equivalent to the value of the goods to the Customs and requests the Customs to release the goods;
(4) where the Customs considers that the consignee or consignor has sufficient evidence to prove that the goods do not infringe the intellectual property rights of the intellectual property right holder; or
(5) where the intellectual property right holder withdraws the application for detention of the suspected infringing goods before the Customs determines that the detained suspected infringing goods are infringing goods.
